  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-8562" textLabel="purple acid phosphatase [multifunctional]" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-8562" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="915" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment source="PMN, dreher">PAP12 functions as a phosphate scavenger for plants during instances of nutritional phosphate deprivation |CITS: [20545876]|. Similar to the PAP26 protein, the PAP12 isozyme exhibits optimal activity at a pH of 5.6 and is able to act on various substrates |CITS: [20545876]|. PAP12 exists as a secreted glycosylated homodimer. A 37-amino acid signal peptide is cleaved from PAP12 |CITS: [20545876]|. PAP12 is able to act on various substrates, with the highest level of activity attained using PEP (treated as 100% activity). Lower levels of activity were seen using phosphotyrosine (60%), |FRAME:D-glucose-6-phosphate D-glucose-6-phosphate| (40%), |FRAME:RIBOSE-5P D-ribose 5-phosphate| (9%), phosphothreonine (29%),(19%), |FRAME:ATP ATP| (81%), |FRAME:ADP ADP| (68%), |FRAME:GTP GTP| (45%), |FRAME:DAMP dAMP| (12%), and |FRAME:AMP AMP| (9%). It did not show activity against phytic acid or phosphocholine, and |FRAME:FRUCTOSE-6P D-fructose 6-phosphate| |CITS: [20545876]|. PAP12 is heavily controlled at the transcriptional level and there is a strong correlation between the PAP12 transcript levels and the secreted PAP12 polypeptide levels |CITS: [20545876]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-8562" />
